On Dec 12, 3:06 pm, sreservoir <sreserv...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Hmmm...  what if the Reshaper also had the ability to
> > summon nasties?

Or maybe just the ability to summon 3d5 Yora Hawks.
*That* certainly ought to give people second thoughts
about deliberately stripping down to skivvies, leaving
all their inventory on another level, and casually fooling
around with a reshaper in an attempt to get a spare
pair of +7 Elvish boots converted into more exotic
footwear, or whatever.

I don't mind if it's theoretically *possible* to take
advantage of their reshaping ability, but there should
be a lot of risk involved, because they're meant to
be primarily a *threat* to your inventory, a sort of
big brother to the rust monster and disenchanter.

> SMOP issues aside, it would be somewhat thematically
> appropriate for it to be a shapechanger.

Or to change *you* into a shapechanger (a chameleon, say),
instead of reshaping your inventory, some percentage of the time.

It would also be thematically appropriate for the ring of protection
from shapechangers to be immune from reshaping (and also not
to be produced thereby), but I'm not sure that's really necessary.

If I wanted to be really evil, I'd suggest an even higher-threat
member of the class, R, Disintegrator, a creature who just plain
outright destroys one item from your inventory each time he
hits you, starting with armor (armor that's not covered by other
armor going first), followed if he continues hitting you after
you're armorless by wielded items next, followed then by
whatever else you might happen to be carrying.  If he should
ever happen to hit you when you have *nothing* in your
inventory, you die, unless you have disintegration resistance.
Anything you throw at him would disintegrate, of course, so
the only ways to harm him would be in melee (which lets
him destroy stuff) or with wands or spells.  Oh, and he would
probably be resistant to stoning, just to make it fair.


